WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.420
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.955
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.255 --> 00:00:17.190
Unit thirty: Negotiation and Diplomacy. In this unit, you will discuss how conflicts are resolved through negotiation.

4
00:00:18.183 --> 00:00:19.405
diplomatic channel.

5
00:00:20.105 --> 00:00:25.181
diplomatic channel means an official route of communication between governments.

6
00:00:25.481 --> 00:00:28.270
The dispute was resolved through diplomatic channels.

7
00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:32.226
Your turn. Say: diplomatic channel.

8
00:00:34.926 --> 00:00:36.392
diplomatic channel.

9
00:00:37.383 --> 00:00:38.617
bargaining power.

10
00:00:39.317 --> 00:00:44.219
bargaining power means the ability to influence the terms of an agreement.

11
00:00:44.519 --> 00:00:48.596
Large economies have more bargaining power in trade talks.

12
00:00:49.296 --> 00:00:52.460
Your turn. Say: bargaining power.

13
00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.439
bargaining power.

14
00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:58.498
concession.

15
00:00:59.198 --> 00:01:02.892
concession means something you agree to give up to reach an agreement.

16
00:01:03.192 --> 00:01:05.900
Both sides made concessions to end the strike.

17
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.055
Your turn. Say: concession.

18
00:01:11.755 --> 00:01:12.826
concession.

19
00:01:13.817 --> 00:01:14.818
stalemate.

20
00:01:15.518 --> 00:01:20.025
stalemate means a situation where neither side can make progress.

21
00:01:20.325 --> 00:01:22.952
The negotiations ended in stalemate.

22
00:01:23.652 --> 00:01:26.432
Your turn. Say: stalemate.

23
00:01:29.132 --> 00:01:30.168
stalemate.

24
00:01:31.183 --> 00:01:32.301
mediation.

25
00:01:33.001 --> 00:01:37.485
mediation means help from a neutral third party to settle a dispute.

26
00:01:37.785 --> 00:01:41.212
Mediation by a neighbouring country helped both sides.

27
00:01:41.912 --> 00:01:44.332
Your turn. Say: mediation.

28
00:01:47.032 --> 00:01:47.929
mediation.

29
00:01:48.917 --> 00:01:50.417
bilateral talks.

30
00:01:51.117 --> 00:01:54.730
bilateral talks means discussions between two countries.

31
00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:57.808
The leaders held bilateral talks on trade.

32
00:01:58.508 --> 00:02:02.263
Your turn. Say: bilateral talks.

33
00:02:04.963 --> 00:02:06.417
bilateral talks.

34
00:02:07.417 --> 00:02:08.720
multilateralism.

35
00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.612
multilateralism means cooperation between many countries on shared problems.

36
00:02:14.912 --> 00:02:18.235
ASEAN is an example of regional multilateralism.

37
00:02:18.935 --> 00:02:22.597
Your turn. Say: multilateralism.

38
00:02:25.297 --> 00:02:26.728
multilateralism.

39
00:02:27.717 --> 00:02:28.880
soft power.

40
00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:34.517
soft power means influence through culture and values rather than force.

41
00:02:34.817 --> 00:02:38.117
K-pop has increased South Korea's soft power.

42
00:02:38.817 --> 00:02:41.911
Your turn. Say: soft power.

43
00:02:44.611 --> 00:02:45.774
soft power.

44
00:02:46.783 --> 00:02:48.992
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:49.292 --> 00:02:53.114
Ms. Sarah, how do countries avoid conflict when they disagree?

46
00:02:54.417 --> 00:02:59.287
First of all, through diplomatic channels. Talking is always cheaper than fighting.

47
00:03:00.583 --> 00:03:03.790
But what if one side has much more bargaining power?

48
00:03:05.083 --> 00:03:11.625
In that case, mediation by a neutral party can help. Moreover, multilateral groups balance power.

49
00:03:12.917 --> 00:03:14.138
Like ASEAN?

50
00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:22.108
Exactly. That said, multilateralism can be slow, and talks sometimes reach stalemate.

51
00:03:23.417 --> 00:03:25.521
Then someone must make concessions.

52
00:03:26.817 --> 00:03:32.418
Indeed. On the whole, the best agreements leave both sides feeling they have gained something.

53
00:03:33.717 --> 00:03:37.040
Grammar focus: Discourse markers in debate.

54
00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:39.780
Discourse markers structure a debate.

55
00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:46.503
First of all introduces your first point; that said concedes a weakness; on the whole concludes.

56
00:03:46.803 --> 00:03:50.614
For example: That said, multilateralism can be slow.

57
00:03:50.914 --> 00:03:55.491
In debates, concession markers like 'that said' make you sound balanced and persuasive.

58
00:03:56.483 --> 00:03:59.391
Now read and listen. The Art of Agreement.

59
00:03:59.691 --> 00:04:03.955
Diplomacy is often described as the art of letting others have your way.

60
00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:10.570
Successful negotiators prepare carefully and understand the other side's interests.

61
00:04:11.883 --> 00:04:17.087
When bargaining power is unequal, mediation can help create a fairer process.

62
00:04:18.383 --> 00:04:23.831
Regional organisations such as ASEAN encourage dialogue through multilateralism.

63
00:04:25.117 --> 00:04:29.032
That said, consensus-based decision-making can make progress slow.

64
00:04:30.317 --> 00:04:35.846
Countries also use soft power, from culture to education, to build influence.

65
00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:41.367
When talks reach stalemate, small concessions can restore trust.

66
00:04:42.683 --> 00:04:48.770
On the whole, history suggests that patient diplomacy prevents more wars than military strength.

67
00:04:50.083 --> 00:04:52.817
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:53.117 --> 00:04:56.637
A situation where neither side can make progress is a blank.

69
00:04:56.937 --> 00:05:00.730
A, concession. B, stalemate. C, mediation.

70
00:05:04.717 --> 00:05:07.099
The answer is B: stalemate.

71
00:05:08.583 --> 00:05:11.570
The plan is expensive. blank, it could save lives.

72
00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:16.707
A, First of all. B, That said. C, In that case.

73
00:05:20.717 --> 00:05:22.658
The answer is B: That said.

74
00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:26.463
blank, I think the agreement is fair.

75
00:05:26.763 --> 00:05:30.834
A, On the whole. B, That said. C, In that case.

76
00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:37.430
The answer is A: On the whole.

77
00:05:38.917 --> 00:05:42.077
Influence through culture rather than force is blank.

78
00:05:42.377 --> 00:05:47.552
A, soft power. B, bargaining power. C, bilateral talks.

79
00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.618
The answer is A: soft power.

80
00:05:56.117 --> 00:06:00.763
According to the text, what can restore trust when talks reach stalemate?

81
00:06:01.063 --> 00:06:07.619
A, military threats. B, small concessions. C, silence.

82
00:06:11.617 --> 00:06:14.568
The answer is B: small concessions.

83
00:06:16.083 --> 00:06:20.349
Now it's your turn. Part one: What makes a good negotiator?

84
00:06:25.649 --> 00:06:31.457
Part two: Use three discourse markers to give two qualities and one example.

85
00:06:37.757 --> 00:06:51.572
First of all, a good negotiator listens carefully. Moreover, they understand what the other side truly needs. That said, they must also know their own limits. On the whole, patience is the most important skill.

86
00:06:51.872 --> 00:06:53.929
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:06:54.917 --> 00:07:00.146
Great work today! You learned eight new words and discourse markers in debate.

88
00:07:00.446 --> 00:07:04.852
Today's value is patience. Wait calmly and take your time.

89
00:07:05.152 --> 00:07:07.978
See you in Unit thirty-one. Goodbye!

90
00:07:08.278 --> 00:07:11.151
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

